EXAMPLE (C, after):  if (len == 0 || len > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(*p)) { /* handle */ } p = malloc(len * sizeof(*p));

- Unsigned integer wrap in arithmetic and allocation size calculations
WHAT: Unsigned integer subtraction or multiplication can wrap around (e.g., when computing buffer sizes or differences), producing a smaller-than-expected result and leading to under-allocation or logic errors.
WHY: Wrapped results can cause heap/stack buffer overflows, memory corruption, and potential remote code execution when used for memory allocation or bounds calculations.
HOW: Before performing unsigned arithmetic on potentially large or user-influenced values, check operands and/or results for wrap conditions (e.g., for subtraction verify result ≤ minuend; for multiplication ensure operand ≤ SIZE_MAX / element_size) and handle errors if checks fail.
